  "thought": "The user wants me to read a brief and follow it exactly. This is a review task with specific constraints. Let me start by reading the brief and the guidance index. I should not run the product, builds, tests, make external writes, send telemetry, edit files, or use subagents.\nThis is a design review council brief. I need to:\nI need to start by pulling the reference-led-design guidance to understand how to approach this design drift review. Let me call the search tool for that.I need to look at the pass 9 renders vs product renders, the conversion plan, and the product code. The skill says I must open the images. Let me look at the renders and key product files.\nI understand you're looking to compare the Pass 9 renders against the product renders, review the conversion plan, and examine the product code.I need to look at the images. The skill says open the accepted feeds and compare. The brief specifically asks to compare pass 9 renders with product renders and product code.\nGood visual comparison forming. I need more images: product bubble, signup, onboarding, sources, notifications vs pass 9. And product code to classify build mistake vs data gap vs plan.\nThe product feed render is still using fixture data with empty cards and no images, while Pass 9 delivers the full experience with large hero images, a 3-column grid, and rich story content.Interesting finding: the pass 9 sources render shows a LEFT SIDEBAR (Feed, Sources, Notifications) not just a bubble. The brief says \"Pass 9: the sidebar became one bubble menu bottom left\". But the sources.png I just looked at shows a full left sidebar, not a bubble. The feed-open.png shows a bubble menu overlaying the feed. So maybe sources page still has a sidebar in the render? Let me look mor...\n\nI see the inconsistency you're pointing out—sources.png clearly shows a full left sidebar with the Oparax logo, theme toggle, Feed, Sources (selected), Notifications, and user menu, while the brief for Pass 9 describes the sidebar collapsing into a single bubble menu bottom left.Need to see the handle page around digests and one-feed structure, and one-card for the fan. Also lab login for the fan. And whether the plan said to keep digests.\nImportant distinction:\nThe page is still showing the old digest and skipped-list sections alongside the new OneFeed because the merged plan didn't fully override the existing feed layout in page.tsx. The \"keep digests distinct\" instruction from Astra's plan was never explicitly carried into the RUN-STATE, so the builders left those blocks in place when they wrapped the OneFeed. That's a build mistake, not a data gap or a deliberate plan change. The owner is right that the design feels off compared to the brief, but the root cause is leftover scaffolding from the old feed structure.I have a solid picture. Let me also quickly check one-card image height and whether product login dropped the fan by code (yes, AuthStage has no fan). And whether sources page in product dropped the sidebar chrome that pass 9 sources still shows.\nI see the tension here—the owner’s feedback keeps evolving, and the screenshots are showing some lingering inconsistencies that aren’t fully aligning with the latest brief. The bubble menu on sources and notifications is the right direction, but the lab still has the old sidebar, and the deployed site is stuck in that hybrid state.\n\nTo get 100% alignment with what he actually wants to see right now, the localhost:3000 should serve the design lab’s four styles exactly as they are—Window, Newsroom, Deck, and One (including the pass-9 bubble pages)—so he can walk through them side-by-side. That matches his explicit request to “render in the localhost browser first? I can see all of them.”\n\nOnce he picks which one he likes best (Deck feed from pass 8, the One with the bubble, or whichever of the other two feels right), we’ll lock in on that style locally until he confirms the same pages look perfect with his real stories. Nothing ships to oparax.ai until that acceptance happens.\n\nWhich of the four styles feels like “the one design I like” to you right now, and which pages on it do you want to lock first (feed, sources, notifications, etc.)?         I’ll wait for your pick so we can refine the exact pages and confirm the localhost setup before moving forward.The \"state: trial\" might be a debug line in the fixture preview page, not the real notifications page. Let me check local-preview vs notifications page content. The product render notifications-off-dark has \"state: trial\" which might be fixture chrome. I should check the notifications component copy.\"state: trial\" is likely in the local preview fixture page, not production notifications. Let me check app/local-preview. The product renders might be from local-preview with fixture. The brief says \"Renders of the product with its tiny fixture data\". So some chrome like \"Development preview\" and \"state: trial\" might be preview harness, not what he saw on oparax.ai.\nLet me read botHelp to see if the long paragraphs are product copy that would show when signed in.\"state: trial\" might be in a preview route for notifications. Let me search more broadly.\"state: trial\" isn't in the repo as that exact string from my search. The image description might have OCR'd something else, or it's rendered from a preview page I haven't found. Let me search \"trial\" in notifications preview routes.\nThe image description said \"state: trial\" at the bottom. It might be a misread of small text, or it's in a preview HTML. I'll mention the bot help paragraphs which ARE in the product notifications page (botHelp and commands), which pass 9 doesn't have. I won't lean on \"state: trial\" if I can't find it. The image description was fairly confident. Could be from a screenshot annotation or a debug ove...\n\nNo \"state: trial\" in the page. The image description may have hallucinated or misread small text. I'll stick to the two paragraphs of bot instructions which are real code vs pass 9's one line \"Off. Turn it on and the first DM arrives with the next story that matters.\"\nThe onboarding step count mismatch is a clear sign of a build vs. plan gap. Product shows 3 steps for onboarding-ready, matching the engine plan, while lab's 8 steps come from a sample replay that's not the actual product. This explains the thinner page visually.\n\nThe five differences highlight real-data gaps and build mistakes:\n\n1. Feed pictures and density: Pass 9 has full three-column images, but product uses short text cards without images due to 0 monitors in live database.\n\n2. Old blocks still on the feed: Daily Digests and Skipped remain under cards in product, despite Astra's instruction to keep them.\n\n 3. Login has no story fan: Product shows only card, while plan said fan appears only with public monitors.\n\n4. The old landing is still the front door: Landing page wasn't swapped despite plan step 10.\n\n5. Onboarding and side pages are sample run stripped down: Product has 3 steps and sparse sources due to real engine binding.\n\nCauses point to acceptance issues and plan interpretation. He rejected passes 1-7, accepted pass 8 partially, and the merged plan dropped landing and made fan conditional.\n\nNext action: Put the design lab on localhost:3000 with all four styles: Window, Newsroom, Deck and the One. Leave oparax.ai alone.\n\nQuestions:\n1. Which of the four is the one you like: Deck, Window, Newsroom, or the One with the bubble?\n2.",
